Hitchhiking is legal in Scotland and no law forbids asking a stranger for a lift; the motorways are the exception, and they have their own regulations rather than the English ones. [https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1995/2507/made The Motorways Traffic (Scotland) Regulations 1995] class pedestrians as excluded traffic and regulation 13 permits them on a motorway only where an accident, an emergency or a vehicle at rest makes it necessary, which rules out the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip roads. [https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-highway-code/motorways-253-to-273 Rule 253 of the Highway Code] states the prohibition and cites these regulations, and rule 271 stops any driver pulling over for you there except in an emergency. Motorway service areas are reached off the motorway and are not part of it, so asking drivers there is lawful, subject to the operator's wishes. Scotland's trunk network is mostly A-road rather than motorway, and on those roads hitchhiking is unregulated — stand off the carriageway where a driver can stop safely.
